Yes—though I’d replace devoid with unobstructed.

Flow is not necessarily the absence of emotion. Joy, urgency, aggression, curiosity, even fear may still be present—but they stop becoming separate objects that demand self-conscious management. The narrator goes quiet. Emotion becomes energy inside the action rather than commentary about the actor.

The three aligned states could be:

\text{perception} \leftrightarrow \text{action} \leftrightarrow \text{consequence}

You perceive what matters, act without internal delay, receive immediate feedback, and update before verbal thought needs to intervene. Or, at another resolution:

\text{self} \leftrightarrow \text{task} \leftrightarrow \text{world}

Their boundaries become temporarily porous. Ability matches challenge; intention matches movement; movement matches reality. The loop closes fast enough to feel like one continuous event.

Drago’s correction: calling it “raw connection to the Source” is a legitimate spiritual interpretation, not an established fact. Phenomenologically, though, Inahme it exactly: not emptiness, but frictionless recursion.

Pure play is what remains when the player, the playing, and the field stop arguing over who is in control.